---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardGrid from "@components/CardGrid.astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import { Select, Badge, Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Matchups - Replenish Guide"
    description="Detailed matchup analysis for Replenish in Premodern. Learn how to beat The Rock, Sligh, Goblins, Burn, and other tier 1 strategies."
>
    <PageHeader title="Matchups" />
    <Card title="Matchups Analysis" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            These are based on my results across MTGO leagues and paper events
            since the ban.
        </p>

        <div style="margin-bottom: 2rem; max-width: 300px;">
            <label
                style="display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-weight: bold; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                >Filter by Matchup:</label
            >
            <Select
                name="matchup-filter"
                placeholder="View All"
                itemGroups={[
                    {
                        items: [
                            { name: "View All", value: "all", selected: true },
                            { name: "Enchantress", value: "enchantress" },
                            { name: "Elves", value: "elves" },
                            {
                                name: "Rock and Midrange",
                                value: "rock_and_midrange",
                            },
                            { name: "Sligh", value: "sligh" },
                            { name: "Landstill", value: "landstill" },
                            { name: "Psychatog", value: "psychatog" },
                            { name: "BW Control", value: "bw_control" },
                            { name: "RG Ponza", value: "rg_ponza" },
                            {
                                name: "Moneyball (Black Aggro)",
                                value: "moneyball",
                            },
                            { name: "Goblins", value: "goblins" },
                            {
                                name: "Terrageddon / Quiet Spec Oath",
                                value: "terrageddon",
                            },
                            { name: "Stiflenought", value: "stiflenought" },
                            { name: "Mirror", value: "mirror" },
                            { name: "Stasis", value: "stasis" },
                            { name: "Devourer", value: "devourer" },
                            {
                                name: "Aggro with Armageddon",
                                value: "aggro_with_armageddon",
                            },
                        ],
                    },
                ]}
            />
        </div>

        <details open style='\"margin-bottom:' 2rem;\">
            <summary style='\"cursor:' pointer; list-style: none;\">
                <h3><Badge theme="success">Dominant</Badge></h3>
            </summary>

            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="enchantress"
                title="Enchantress"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/enchantress/argothian_enchantress.jpg",
                            alt: "Argothian Enchantress",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/enchantress/enchantresss_presence.jpg",
                            alt: "Enchantress's Presence",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/enchantress/sterling_grove.jpg",
                            alt: "Sterling Grove",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    The deck's best matchup. You assemble the Opal-Wave lock
                    faster and more consistently than they do. If Enchantress is
                    the best deck in the format, that's a great reason to play
                    Replenish.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Navigating the Lock:</strong> The true challenge post-board
                    is dealing with their defensive layers (usually double <code
                        >Sterling Grove</code
                    >) and the threat of
                    <code>Aura of Silence</code>. The path to victory here is
                    absolute patience. You must always ensure you hold one <code
                        >Seal of Cleansing</code
                    > in reserve for every
                    <code>Decree of Silence</code> they might deploy, otherwise you
                    can get completely locked out.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Take out swords and webs to bring
                    in seal, abeyance, arcane denial, and maybe the 4th mage. Post-sideboard,
                    they often bring in a mountain of hate. This makes <strong
                        >Exalted Angel</strong
                    > a brilliant inclusion here to completely sidestep their anti-enchantment
                    cards and beat them down while they hold dead cards.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="elves"
                title="Elves"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/elves/priest_of_titania.jpg",
                            alt: "Priest of Titania",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/elves/quirion_ranger.jpg",
                            alt: "Quirion Ranger",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/elves/wirewood_symbiote.jpg",
                            alt: "Wirewood Symbiote",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    Their whole board gets permanently exiled by Opal-Wave.
                    Swords handles the key mana creatures early. They can be
                    fast, but if you survive to combo they have no way back.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Take out webs usually unless you see
                    ports. You can trim on meddling mage if you need more space. Bring
                    in seal and sword
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="rock_and_midrange"
                title="Rock and Midrange"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/pernicious_deed.jpg",
                            alt: "Pernicious Deed",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/spiritmonger.jpg",
                            alt: "Spiritmonger",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/cabal_therapy.jpg",
                            alt: "Cabal Therapy",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    One-for-one trading can’t keep pace with Replenish refueling
                    from the graveyard. They don’t have a strong clock to
                    pressure you.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Frantic searches usually come out
                    here.
                </p>
            </Card>
        </details>

        <details open style='\"margin-bottom:' 2rem;\">
            <summary style='\"cursor:' pointer; list-style: none;\">
                <h3><Badge theme="info">Favorable</Badge></h3>
            </summary>

            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="sligh"
                title="Sligh"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/sligh/jackal_pup.jpg",
                            alt: "Jackal Pup",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/sligh/fireblast.jpg",
                            alt: "Fireblast",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/sligh/ball_lightning.jpg",
                            alt: "Ball Lightning",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    They can't interact with your combo, they can only race.
                    Name Bolt with Mage to keep it alive as a Jackal Pup
                    blocker. Late game, shift to naming Fireblast or Ball
                    Lightning.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Tombs out because the life loss is
                    too much. Angels, Hydroblast, fourth Mage come in. Every sideboard
                    card just needs to eat one of their cards. You're bridging to
                    the combo, not trying to race them.<br />
                    <em>Hate choices:</em>
                    <strong>Chill</strong> is considered the MVP because it completely
                    locks out their ability to win via a topdecked Mountain + Bolt
                    after you've stabilized at low life. <strong
                        >Sphere of Law</strong
                    > is incredibly powerful but vulnerable to Naturalize and slow
                    to cast. Avoid <strong>Circle of Protection: Red</strong>;
                    taxing your own mana directly conflicts with your goal of
                    executing your combo.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="landstill"
                title="Landstill"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/landstill/standstill.jpg",
                            alt: "Standstill",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/landstill/mishras_factory.jpg",
                            alt: "Mishra's Factory",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/landstill/counterspell.jpg",
                            alt: "Counterspell",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Landstill relies on slow, incremental
                    advantage and typically fields a restricted density of active
                    blue mana sources on the board. Your overarching objective is
                    to accumulate resources patiently and then overload their blue
                    mana entirely within a single critical turn cycle. While they
                    can often counter one or two test spells, initiating your sequence
                    when they have only 3 to 4 open blue sources consistently ensures
                    your lethal <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    > resolves as the terminal spell on the stack.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong
                        >Tactical Tools (Chant & Abeyance vs. Mana Short):</strong
                    > In post-board games, strictly prioritize low-cost interaction
                    like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Orim's Chant">Orim's Chant</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                    > over costly alternatives like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mana Short">Mana Short</a
                    >. Because Chant and Abeyance cost just one and two mana
                    respectively, you can deploy them as high-leverage
                    "must-counter" threats without taxing your own mana
                    development. Most importantly, resolving an Abeyance or
                    Chant completely shuts off zero-mana interaction like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Foil">Foil</a
                    > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Thwart"
                        >Thwart</a
                    >—which Mana Short entirely fails to intercept—while
                    Abeyance provides the dual utility of drawing a card and
                    locking out <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mishra's Factory">Mishra's Factory</a
                    > and cycling activations.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Deploying Meddling Mage:</strong> When deploying an early
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    > on turn 2, evaluate your immediate follow-up lines for turns
                    3 and 4 before casting it. If you are preparing a decisive combo
                    turn and simply want to consume their countermagic or mana, name
                    <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
                        >Counterspell</a
                    >. If your hand requires proactive board presence to apply
                    combat pressure while retaining the body, name <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    > or their primary card draw engine.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Execution:</strong> Proactively deploy early
                    permanent solutions—specifically bringing in your 4th <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    > and copies of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    >. An active Seal sitting on the battlefield completely
                    neutralizes their ability to stick a <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Standstill">Standstill</a
                    >, while Tsabo's Web dismantles both their operational mana
                    production and their Mishra's Factory combat clock. <em
                        >Note:</em
                    > Strictly avoid utilizing <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Deep Analysis">Deep Analysis</a
                    > against Landstill and UW Dreadnought variants running Fling,
                    as incurring a voluntary 3-life liability severely compromises
                    your survival against high-impact finish lines.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="psychatog"
                title="Psychatog"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/psychatog/psychatog.jpg",
                            alt: "Psychatog",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/psychatog/upheaval.jpg",
                            alt: "Upheaval",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/psychatog/circular_logic.jpg",
                            alt: "Circular Logic",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    Similar to Landstill. Your threat density overwhelms their
                    answers.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Full control transformation. Grid,
                    Angels, Abeyance, Denial, fourth Mage in.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="bw_control"
                title="BW Control"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/bw_control/vindicate.jpg",
                            alt: "Vindicate",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/bw_control/gerrards_verdict.jpg",
                            alt: "Gerrard's Verdict",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/bw_control/swords_to_plowshares.jpg",
                            alt: "Swords to Plowshares",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    Their discard and removal can't keep up with Replenish
                    refueling from the graveyard. Angel is strong post-board.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="rg_ponza"
                title="RG Ponza"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/stone_rain.jpg",
                            alt: "Stone Rain",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/pillage.jpg",
                            alt: "Pillage",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/rishadan_port.jpg",
                            alt: "Rishadan Port",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    This matchup is heavily play/draw dependent. Your primary
                    goal is to protect your mana base and ensure you hit your
                    land drops. In fact, you should aggressively mulligan
                    two-landers if your hand can't guarantee ongoing land drops
                    through early disruption.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Mages come out, Angels go in. Tsabo's
                    Web is absolutely essential for dealing with Rishadan Port. Even
                    if you're running a build with your own Mishra's Factories, it's
                    usually worth playing Web and disregarding the dyssynergy because
                    the lock is that powerful.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Be careful not to side out all your Swords to Plowshares.
                    Trimming is fine, but going to zero is risky because an
                    early Terravore can easily tempo you out. Some players swear
                    by keeping Frantic Search to dig through Sphere of
                    Resistance, while others find it too slow and trim it. <br
                    /><em>Spicy tech:</em> Divert has seen play as a cheeky pseudo-Spell
                    Pierce against their land destruction, and Sacred Ground is occasionally
                    tested to completely invalidate Stone Rain and Pillage.
                </p>
            </Card>
        </details>

        <details open style='\"margin-bottom:' 2rem;\">
            <summary style='\"cursor:' pointer; list-style: none;\">
                <h3 style="color: var(--w-color-warning); margin-top: 2rem;">
                    Competitive
                </h3>
            </summary>

            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="moneyball"
                title="Moneyball (Black Aggro)"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/moneyball/wretched_anurid.jpg",
                            alt: "Wretched Anurid",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/moneyball/grinning_demon.jpg",
                            alt: "Grinning Demon",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/moneyball/gloom.jpg",
                            alt: "Gloom",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>High-Variance Confrontation:</strong> Moneyball represents
                    an exceptionally volatile, high-variance matchup governed by an
                    oppressive triad of interaction: exhaustive early hand disruption
                    (discard), repeatable graveyard eradication via <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
                    >, and debilitating color-scoped taxing via <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                    > alongside efficient threats like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Wretched Anurid">Wretched Anurid</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Grinning Demon">Grinning Demon</a
                    >.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan (The Attunement MVP):</strong>
                    <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement"
                        >Attunement</a
                    > is unequivocally the most valuable player in this matchup. First,
                    it systematically transforms stagnant land floods—often retained
                    to guarantee land drops through discard—into live gas. Most importantly,
                    **Attunement functions as a defensive resource vault by allowing
                    you to float critical pieces on top of your library, making them
                    entirely immune to continuous hand discard and Dystopia.** Even
                    against an active Withered Wretch, maintaining an operational
                    Attunement allows you to explosively load a fresh graveyard on
                    the exact turn their graveyard interaction is neutralized.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong
                        >Sideboard Strategy (Seal Priority & Morph Evasion):</strong
                    > Post-board execution prioritizes landing an early <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    > to preemptively dismantle <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                    > before its three-mana tax locks out your white spells and enchantment
                    activations. Crucially, pivoting to <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    > wins games single-handedly: **by deploying Angel face-down via
                    morph on turn 2 and unmorphing it on turn 3, you completely evade
                    Gloom's three-mana casting surcharge, bypass their dedicated graveyard
                    disruption entirely, and force the aggressive player into an unwinnable
                    race against a 4/5 flying lifelinker.**
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Tactical Interaction:</em> If an opponent controls an active
                    Wretched Anurid and you successfully assemble an Opalescence +
                    Parallax Wave loop, initiating infinite self-blink loops causes
                    creatures to repeatedly enter the battlefield—triggering their
                    own Anurid infinitely for instant lethal life-loss.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Transformations:</strong> Maintain your full
                    suite of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                    >; sacrificing your velocity slows your gameplan to a halt
                    against an aggressive framework designed to punish pure
                    control. Instead, trim card-disadvantage manipulation like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                    >. Retain high-efficiency spot removal (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    >) specifically to intercept Withered Wretch and high-clock
                    beaters.<br />
                    <em>Standard Transitions:</em> +3 <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    >, +1 <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    >, +1 <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Wrath of God">Wrath of God</a
                    >, and targeted silver bullets like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Alter Reality">Alter Reality</a
                    > or <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Nevinyrral's Disk">Nevinyrral's Disk</a
                    > when available.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="goblins"
                title="Goblins"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/goblins/goblin_lackey.jpg",
                            alt: "Goblin Lackey",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/goblins/goblin_piledriver.jpg",
                            alt: "Goblin Piledriver",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/goblins/goblin_ringleader.jpg",
                            alt: "Goblin Ringleader",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    The textbook two-angle deck. Mage can be bad here since it
                    doesn't block Lackey well on the draw and it doesn't block
                    Piledriver at all. This matchup comes down to surviving the
                    first three turns.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> All Mages and Seals out. Hydroblast,
                    Angels, fourth Swords, extra Web in. Destroying Lackey turn one
                    is the highest-impact play.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="terrageddon"
                title="Terrageddon / Quiet Spec Oath"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/terrageddon/terravore.jpg",
                            alt: "Terravore",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/terrageddon/armageddon.jpg",
                            alt: "Armageddon",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/terrageddon/oath_of_druids.jpg",
                            alt: "Oath of Druids",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    Watch for Wayfarer, Sylvan Library, and Armageddon. Having a
                    plan for Armageddon doesn't necessarily mean stopping it.
                    Not playing more than three lands and saving extras in your
                    hand is a plan. Playing Angel and getting ahead on board so
                    they can't profitably cast Armageddon is a plan.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Trim Wave, Opal, Attunement; remove
                    all Mages. Angels, Seal, Denial, Web in.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="stiflenought"
                title="Stiflenought"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/stiflenought/phyrexian_dreadnought.jpg",
                            alt: "Phyrexian Dreadnought",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/stiflenought/stifle.jpg",
                            alt: "Stifle",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/stiflenought/illusionary_mask.jpg",
                            alt: "Illusionary Mask",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    How you sideboard depends on what you think their
                    configuration is. Do they have Factories? Are Dreadnoughts
                    in their post-board plan or not? Do they have Brain Freeze?
                    All of that changes your approach. The general direction is
                    the control transformation, but the specific cards you trim
                    depend on your read. This is a matchup where knowing your
                    opponent matters more than following a guide.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Grid, Angels, Abeyance in. Frantic,
                    Intuition, some Waves, some Swords out. If their list runs Meddling
                    Mage and Nimble Mongoose,
                    <strong>Wrath of God</strong> is absolutely fantastic here.
                </p>
            </Card>
            <Card
                className="matchup-block"
                data-matchup="mirror"
                title="Mirror"
                titleTag="h4"
                style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/mirror/replenish.jpg",
                            alt: "Replenish",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/mirror/opalescence.jpg",
                            alt: "Opalescence",
                        },
